## Mechanism (read at upstream/main d99fc204)

`AutodetectProcessManager.flushJob`:

    communicator.flushJob(params, (flushAcknowledgement, e) -> {
        if (e != null) {
            String msg = String.format(Locale.ROOT, "[%s] exception while flushing job", jobTask.getJobId());
            logger.error(msg);                                   // no throwable
            handler.onFailure(ExceptionsHelper.serverError(msg, e));
        }

`AutodetectCommunicator.submitOperation` hands the handler a `conflictStatusException("[{}] Could not submit operation to process as it has been killed")` when `processKilled` is set. That is an expected outcome when a job is closed with force, deleted, or its persistent task is removed while a flush is queued. It is reported as ERROR, and the cause is not attached to the log line (it is attached only to the REST response). `forecastJob` right below passes `e` to `logger.error`, so the two are inconsistent.

## Impact

One ERROR per queued flush during a node or task disruption; the line carries no reason, so it cannot be told apart from a real native-process failure. ERROR streams are used for alerting.

## Proposal

- Log the throwable (`logger.error(msg, e)`) for non-conflict failures.
- For the killed conflict (status 409 from the "has been killed" path) log at DEBUG/INFO or not at all; the caller already receives the failure.

Scope: one method, plus a unit test in `AutodetectProcessManagerTests`.

